1
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:08,131
Imagine a satellite taking pictures of the Earth at night, or through

2
00:00:08,131 --> 00:00:08,900
thick clouds.

3
00:00:08,950 --> 00:00:13,404
Normal cameras cannot do that, but radar can. This kind of radar is

4
00:00:13,404 --> 00:00:16,062
called Synthetic Aperture Radar, or SAR.

5
00:00:16,112 --> 00:00:20,143
The catch is that SAR images are very noisy. They are covered in a

6
00:00:20,143 --> 00:00:21,975
grainy pattern called speckle.

7
00:00:22,025 --> 00:00:25,890
Our task, from the Thales challenge, is to find the vehicle in each

8
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:29,812
radar image, and mark every pixel as vehicle, shadow, or background.

9
00:00:29,862 --> 00:00:32,200
This is called image segmentation.

10
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:36,319
To do it well, every pixel must be judged together with its neighbours,

11
00:00:36,319 --> 00:00:40,446
and that turns into a giant puzzle with more possible answers than atoms

12
00:00:40,446 --> 00:00:41,363
in the universe.

13
00:00:41,412 --> 00:00:45,294
Classical computers struggle with puzzles like this, so we asked a

14
00:00:45,294 --> 00:00:47,940
simple question: can a quantum computer help?

15
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:51,120
Here is our idea, in simple words.

16
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:55,588
Each pixel becomes a tiny magnet that points up or down. Up means

17
00:00:55,588 --> 00:00:57,695
vehicle, down means background.

18
00:00:57,745 --> 00:01:01,542
Bright pixels want to point up, and neighbouring magnets like to point

19
00:01:01,542 --> 00:01:03,982
the same way, which keeps the picture smooth.

20
00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:07,778
The best arrangement of all the magnets is the one with the lowest

21
00:01:07,778 --> 00:01:10,333
energy, and it gives us the cleanest picture.

22
00:01:10,383 --> 00:01:14,523
Finding that lowest energy is exactly what a quantum algorithm called

23
00:01:14,523 --> 00:01:18,482
QAOA is designed for, and we run it on Classiq's quantum platform.

24
00:01:18,532 --> 00:01:22,586
One image has four thousand pixels, which is far too many qubits for

25
00:01:22,586 --> 00:01:24,195
today's quantum simulators.

26
00:01:24,245 --> 00:01:28,834
So we cut the image into small three by three tiles of nine qubits each,

27
00:01:28,834 --> 00:01:32,850
solve every tile with QAOA, and stitch the tiles back together.

28
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:35,775
Now let's walk through the project.

29
00:01:35,825 --> 00:01:40,313
This is our live web app, deployed on the internet at quantumsar dot

30
00:01:40,313 --> 00:01:42,888
onrender dot com, so anyone can try it.

31
00:01:42,938 --> 00:01:46,830
At the top, you see the main idea: the image energy becomes a quantum

32
00:01:46,830 --> 00:01:50,610
Hamiltonian, the kind of equation a quantum computer can work with.

33
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:56,300
In the live demo section, I pick one of our fifty sample radar images.

34
00:01:56,350 --> 00:02:01,150
I keep the quantum solver, QAOA on Classiq, and press Run segmentation.

35
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:05,169
The answer appears in about a second, because this image was already

36
00:02:05,169 --> 00:02:07,738
solved on Classiq, and the result was saved.

37
00:02:07,788 --> 00:02:11,603
The blue badge always tells you which engine produced the result, so

38
00:02:11,603 --> 00:02:12,612
nothing is hidden.

39
00:02:12,662 --> 00:02:16,542
The orange shape is the vehicle we found, and it matches the true

40
00:02:16,542 --> 00:02:19,288
answer, shown in the middle, almost perfectly.

41
00:02:19,338 --> 00:02:23,803
Below, you can see the scores, and the quantum circuit used for every

42
00:02:23,803 --> 00:02:27,750
tile: nine qubits, with the circuit size reported by Classiq.

43
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:33,441
Now I switch on three classes. The app also finds the dark radar shadow

44
00:02:33,441 --> 00:02:35,530
behind the vehicle, shown in blue.

45
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:40,130
And with edge detection switched on, it draws a clean, one pixel wide

46
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:43,690
outline: red for the vehicle, and cyan for the shadow.

47
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:49,291
To be fair, we compare the quantum method with classical methods. One

48
00:02:49,291 --> 00:02:51,898
click runs every solver on the same image.

49
00:02:51,948 --> 00:02:56,543
The quantum result reaches the same best energy as graph cut, the exact

50
00:02:56,543 --> 00:03:01,074
classical method, and it is far more accurate than simple thresholding

51
00:03:01,074 --> 00:03:02,498
like Otsu and k-means.

52
00:03:02,548 --> 00:03:06,441
But classical methods are still much faster today, and we show that

53
00:03:06,441 --> 00:03:08,185
honestly in the runtime chart.

54
00:03:08,235 --> 00:03:11,520
All of these numbers can be exported as a CSV file.

55
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:15,155
You can also upload your own image.

56
00:03:15,205 --> 00:03:19,629
This image has never been solved before, so pressing Run sends it live

57
00:03:19,629 --> 00:03:20,830
to Classiq's cloud.

58
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,424
A progress bar shows every stage: first a quick pass on a smaller copy

59
00:03:25,424 --> 00:03:28,280
of the image, then the full resolution pass.

60
00:03:28,330 --> 00:03:32,093
While it runs, the How it works section explains the method in four

61
00:03:32,093 --> 00:03:32,430
steps.

62
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:36,831
And here we list our honest limitations: today's qubit limits keep the

63
00:03:36,831 --> 00:03:40,933
tiles small, and the quantum part is not yet faster than classical

64
00:03:40,933 --> 00:03:41,430
methods.

65
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:50,711
The job has finished. The green badge confirms that this result was

66
00:03:50,711 --> 00:03:53,312
computed live on Classiq, in about a minute.

67
00:03:53,362 --> 00:03:57,631
The result is now saved, so the same image will load instantly next

68
00:03:57,631 --> 00:03:57,950
time.

69
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:01,550
So how did we build and deploy all this?

70
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:06,306
The quantum part uses the Classiq Python SDK. We write the QAOA circuit

71
00:04:06,306 --> 00:04:11,012
in Python, Classiq turns it into a real quantum circuit, and runs it on

72
00:04:11,012 --> 00:04:12,338
its cloud simulator.

73
00:04:12,388 --> 00:04:16,163
We checked it carefully: on small tiles, the quantum answer matches a

74
00:04:16,163 --> 00:04:19,938
full brute force search, and we have more than forty automatic tests.

75
00:04:19,988 --> 00:04:24,315
The classical comparison methods, graph cut, simulated annealing and

76
00:04:24,315 --> 00:04:26,925
thresholding, are also written in Python.

77
00:04:26,975 --> 00:04:31,037
The backend is FastAPI, the website is built in React with animated

78
00:04:31,037 --> 00:04:34,856
React Bits components, and everything is packed into one Docker

79
00:04:34,856 --> 00:04:35,462
container.

80
00:04:35,513 --> 00:04:39,925
Our code is public on GitHub. Every change is tested automatically with

81
00:04:39,925 --> 00:04:44,338
GitHub Actions, and then deployed to Render, where the app runs online.

82
00:04:44,388 --> 00:04:48,324
Classiq normally needs a browser login, so we created a separate, secure

83
00:04:48,324 --> 00:04:52,205
login just for the server, stored as a secret, so the live site can run

84
00:04:52,205 --> 00:04:53,463
quantum jobs by itself.

85
00:04:53,513 --> 00:04:57,685
Long quantum runs are handled as background jobs, one at a time, and

86
00:04:57,685 --> 00:05:01,550
every finished result is saved so it loads instantly next time.

87
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:07,321
QuantumSAR was built by Team From Nowhere: Jerrick Austin Manuel G,

88
00:05:07,321 --> 00:05:12,295
Nidhin Suresh, Suresh Ramanujam, Yokeshwaran Anandan, and Hari Babu.

89
00:05:12,345 --> 00:05:16,107
You can find our code on GitHub and try the live app using the links on

90
00:05:16,107 --> 00:05:17,750
screen. Thank you for watching!
